virglrenderer-macos-venus-transport.patch
=========================================

Three fixes that let virglrenderer's Venus render server start on macOS.
They apply on top of the startergo/virglrenderer tap's macOS patch series,
which already contains the length-prefixed framing these depend on.

1-2. src/proxy/proxy_socket.c, server/render_socket.c

     The tap's series added framing for platforms without SOCK_SEQPACKET and
     wrote the socket-type selection as:

         #ifdef __APPLE__
            int type = SOCK_SEQPACKET;
         #else
            int type = SOCK_SEQPACKET;
         #endif

     Both branches are identical, so the Apple branch never took effect and
     socketpair() failed with EPROTONOSUPPORT — Darwin's AF_UNIX has no
     SOCK_SEQPACKET. QEMU then aborted virgl initialization during guest
     firmware init, so a VM with venus=on never booted at all.

3.   server/render_worker.c

     create_sigchld_fd() treated a failed fcntl(F_SETFL, O_NONBLOCK) on a
     kqueue descriptor as fatal. kqueue descriptors do not support F_GETFL or
     F_SETFL, so this always failed, the worker jail was never created, and
     the render server died immediately after spawning. The flag is also
     unnecessary: the only kevent() wait in that file already passes an
     explicit timeout.

Status
------
With these applied, Venus starts: the render server spawns, the guest's Venus
context is created, and blob resources are created. Venus is still not usable
on Apple Silicon, for a reason none of this addresses — see the README section
"Venus, and why it cannot run on macOS".
